Polymorphism

- Polymorphism means “many forms.” The same action can behave differently
    depending on who does it.
- Real-life example: You say “make a sound” to a dog, a cat, and a cow.
    Same instruction, three different results. You don’t need a separate
    command for each animal.

Java has two kinds.
    1. Overloading (decided when you compile)
    Same method name, different inputs, in the same class.
    2. Overriding (decided while the program runs)
    A child class gives its own version of a parent’s method.

Why backend developers care

    interface Payment { void pay(double amount); }

    class UpiPayment implements Payment {
        public void pay(double amount) { System.out.println("UPI: " + amount); }
    }
    class CardPayment implements Payment {
        public void pay(double amount) { System.out.println("Card: " + amount); }
    }

    class CheckoutService {
        void checkout(Payment p, double amt) { p.pay(amt); }
    }

Quick comparison
	                Overloading	            Overriding
